I keep getting "cannot read properties of undefined (reading \"length\")" in this JavaScript code. Can you help me figure out why?

```javascript
const words = document.getElementById("words");
console.log(words.length);
```

final_entries.response:
This error means that `document.getElementById("words")` is returning `null` — there is no element with the id "words" in your DOM at the time this code runs. There are a few common causes:

1. **Timing issue**: Your script might be running before the DOM is fully loaded. Try wrapping your code in a DOMContentLoaded listener:

```javascript
document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", () => {
    const words = document.getElementById("words");
    console.log(words.length);
});
```

2. **Wrong ID**: Double-check that your HTML actually has an element with `id="words"`.

3. **Dynamic content**: If the element is added dynamically, you need to wait until it exists before accessing it.

A good debugging practice is to add a null check:

```javascript
const words = document.getElementById("words");
if (words === null) {
    console.error("Element with id words not found!");
} else {
    console.log(words.length);
}
```

